A||N||19||danny boy|| Shotguns/skeet shooting||||1||
Z||000000||danny boy||12-23-2001||10:55 PM|||| My grandfather just gave me the very first gun I have ever owned. Its a H&R model 349 Bolt action 16 gauge. I'm having a blast(no pun intended) with it. I'm getting bored shooting at cans, bottles, neighbor kids, and I absolutely will not kill anything with it, so I'd like to get into skeet shooting.<BR> Yes, I know, a bolt action shotgun isn't exactly the greatest skeet gun, especially for a left hander, but thats what I have to start with. <BR> If anyone could give me a low down on this sport I'd appreciate it. I went and looked at a few semi auto 20 gauges, so thats what I'll prolly end up with, so any help with what makes a good 20ga skeet gun would help, too.<BR> One last thing, My gun has threads on the outside of the barrel, and I cannot find a choke for it. Being a 16ga its a hard item to find. What difference does a choke make anyway?||||reg||1
Z||000001||2M4 Dale||12-24-2001||02:52 AM||||The external threads are for a choke/muzzle brake system. Email me I may be able to find the parts you need. As for a good skeet gun...buy a good over-under double barrel with modified clyinder or clyinder bore choke tubes. Plus you don't want a long barrel........Now if your shooting trap the rules change and your H&R would be just fine with a modified or full choke tube....you'll want a longer barrel for trap.||||reg||7
Z||000002||MinnGreenGT||12-24-2001||11:14 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 2M4 Dale:<BR><B>The external threads are for a choke/muzzle brake system. Email me I may be able to find the parts you need. As for a good skeet gun...buy a good over-under double barrel with modified clyinder or clyinder bore choke tubes. Plus you don't want a long barrel........Now if your shooting trap the rules change and your H&R would be just fine with a modified or full choke tube....you'll want a longer barrel for trap.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yup, what he said! I used to work at a skeet/trap/sporting clays range (Minnesota Horse & Hunt Club).  I'd seeing a 16 gauge was certainly rare.  I'd say at least 97% of shooters were using 12 gauges (and that's what I'd prefer).  A nice O/U is the preference for sporting clays/skeet, but a semi or pump is nice too (and usually less expensive).  Advice... just take your time, learn your gun- be comfortable with it.  And if you aren't a natural at it- take a quick lesson (most ranges have a "pro" on staff for lessons) and go from there.  I'm right there with you- I love guns (pretty much all kinds), but I'm no hunter.<P>------------------<BR>              <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/GreenGT6.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||REDHOTT88||12-24-2001||12:04 PM||||OOOOHHHH... wouldn't want to hurt a poor helpless animal!!!<P>You young-uns seem to forget that when this country was founded, there were not any SUPERMARKETS around. <P>Yes, people actually had to KILL animals to survive... and you can't find rabbit, pheasant or deer in the local market.<P>If you think hunting is bad, I'd like to take you to the farmer who produces your "nicely packaged" meat.<P>Don't forget your roots,lest we become a nation of wussy's!!!!<P><P>------------------<BR>                                                                                                         <BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.stopstart.fsnet.co.uk/smilie/FIREdevil.gif">           <FONT COLOR="ffoooo"> <B>1988 Formula 2.8L</B> V6 5 speed  </FONT><BR><FONT SIZE="1">(Runs smooth, shifts crisp, red and shiny all over!)</FONT>||||reg||8
Z||000004||2M4 Dale||12-24-2001||12:41 PM||||I've been a firearms dealer for 10 years now. I cater mostly to the self defense crowd....I used to hunt but don't anymore. We all need to remember that firearms have many GOOD and FUN uses and that we all see those uses in a different light. Myself, I just love killin' the hec outa pop cans and clay pigeons. I see nothing wrong with hunting as a sport or for food. I just don't have the heart for it anymore. But then someone has to kill those hamburgers and rib eyes for us ... don't they !!!<P>I got a good friend who gave me some venison....I think I'll go burn a deer steak for lunch !!!<P>By the way.....I can't hit the broadside of a barn with a shotgun...but I'm a terror with my Berreta !!!||||reg||6
Z||000005||REDHOTT88||12-24-2001||12:45 PM||||I'll get it!!!<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.stopstart.fsnet.co.uk/smilie/snipersmilie.gif">         <IMG SRC="http://www.stopstart.fsnet.co.uk/mica/FoodBurger.gif">  <P><BR>2m4dale.. you're alright!!!<BR>------------------<BR>                                                                                                         <BR>          <IMG SRC="http://www.stopstart.fsnet.co.uk/smilie/FIREdevil.gif">              <FONT COLOR="ffoooo"> <B>1988 Formula 2.8L</B> V6 5 speed  </FONT><BR><FONT SIZE="1">(Runs smooth, shifts crisp, red and shiny all over!)</FONT><P><p>[This message has been edited by REDHOTT88 (edited 12-24-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||avengador1||12-24-2001||01:35 PM||||The choke controls the spread pattern. The more open the choke the larger the spread pattern of the pelletes will be. I have a Mossberg 12 gauge pump with an adjustable choke. Remenber to seat the gun fully to your shoulder before shooting, especially if you're usind magnum loads. When I got my shootgun many moons ago I learned this the hard way and had a black and blue shoulder for a month. I also learned that magnum loads arent good for skeet. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Avendgador1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||MinnGreenGT||12-24-2001||06:35 PM||||RedHott88... you're jumping to conclusions now.  I never said, nor did I indicate that I didn't think hunting was good/safe/whatever as you made it out to be.  I'm all for hunting, and eating meat and everything... I just don't personally feel the need to go sit in a swamp for days at a time shooting ducks, or freeze my ass off sitting in a deerstand for hours.  What's the frickin' point? I can go buy my duck from a reputable meat market cleaned and ready to eat.  We allow people to deer hunt on my parent's property... in trade for a small amount of deer meat (mmmmm... venison).<P>I enjoy meeting up with my other "gun friends" (some are hunters, other just "enthusiasts") and spending the afternoon shooting our weapons, trying other people's new guns, and just hanging out.<P>You really need to think before you go off spouting about "roots" and a "nation of wussies". I own an original colt peacemaker (.45-40 also known as .45WCF... as if you knew what that was), a few .22s, a serial number matched German Luger (7.65mm), and an AR15 (not yet complete due to lack of funds).  Just 'cause I don't hunt doesn't mean I don't want others to hunt.  Oh and your comparison of a meat butcher and a hunter is a load of BS. I don't care how many chickens, turkeys, pigs, or cows are slaughtered every year to put food on my plate.  Go preach it to the veagans.  "Save the trees, eat a vegetarian"  ...or "save a tree, eat a beaver  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">"<P><B><FONT COLOR="green">Peace and Joy on this Christmas!</FONT></B><p>[This message has been edited by MinnGreenGT (edited 12-24-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||LarryB||12-25-2001||04:38 AM||||Depending on what you want, 2 good guns to start your search are a Browning Citori over/under or a Remington 11/87 semiautomatic; each of them has the ability for you to quickly swap out chokes depending on how you'd be using the gun that day.  The Citori would be a bit easier for skeet or doubles trap shooting b/c of the easier control of shell ejection and it also makes a fine gun for upland bird hunting, the 11-87 is a better choice if you're sensitive to recoil, you'd also like to try duck/goose hunting or you live in an area where deer hunting requires a shotgun, and it's cheaper than a Citori.  Each of these are fine, well-made guns you'd be proud to own. <P>Both of them also would make a fine gun for shooting trap as well BTW.<P>In either case, stick with the 12 ga. for now with a gun that will shoot both 2 3/4" and 3" shells, because it's much more versatile than the 16 or 20 ga.  20 ga. really isn't appropriate for skeet (or trap) and 16 ga. ammunition is harder to come across, because it's not a commonly-made caliber shotgun any more.  You don't need a specialized skeet gun until you're hard-core, and if it comes to that you'd probably be spending your money on reloading equipment first. <P>If you have a local gun club with a skeet range, you might want to drop by on a Sunday morning to talk to some of the regulars.  I'm sure they would be glad to share their opinions, and if you're lucky they might even spend a few minutes and let you try a few rounds with their guns.<P>As for vegans, not all of them are anti-hunters.  I've several friends who are vegans who are also strong supporters of hunting rights; they just prefer primarily for holistic purposes to avoid eating any kind of animal-derived foods. <P>And yes, you don't want to use magnum loads for skeet shooting; skeet calls for milder loads with smaller shot.  In a shotgun, magnum loads have more shot by weight and they shoot it with the same or slower speed than a standard load.   Magnum loads are more appropriate for things like goose or duck hunting. <P>Merry Christmas to all! <p>[This message has been edited by LarryB (edited 12-25-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||LarryB||12-25-2001||04:50 AM||||[QUOTE]Originally posted by LarryB:<BR>[B]Depending on what you want, 2 good guns to start your search are a Browning Citori over/under or a Remington 11/87 semiautomatic; each of them has the ability for you to quickly swap out chokes depending on how you'd be using the gun that day.  The Citori would be a bit easier for skeet or doubles trap shooting b/c of the easier control of shell ejection and it also makes a fine gun for upland bird hunting, the 11-87 is a better choice if you're sensitive to recoil, you'd also like to try duck/goose hunting or you live in an area where deer hunting requires a shotgun, and it's cheaper than a Citori.  Each of these are fine, well-made guns you'd be proud to own. The Citori pretty much by design is ambidextrous, and the 11-87 is available in left-hand models. (I also shoot lefty-handed.)<P>Both of them also would make a fine gun for shooting trap as well BTW.<P>In either case, stick with the 12 ga. for now with a gun that will shoot both 2 3/4" and 3" shells, because it's much more versatile than the 16 or 20 ga.  20 ga. really isn't appropriate for skeet (or trap) and 16 ga. ammunition is harder to come across, because it's not a commonly-made caliber shotgun any more.  You don't need a specialized skeet gun until you're hard-core, and if it comes to that you'd probably be spending your money on reloading equipment first. <P>If you have a local gun club with a skeet range, you might want to drop by on a Sunday morning to talk to some of the regulars.  I'm sure they would be glad to share their opinions, and if you're lucky they might even spend a few minutes and let you try a few rounds with their guns.<P>As for vegans, not all of them are anti-hunters.  I've several friends who are vegans who are also strong supporters of hunting rights; they just prefer primarily for holistic purposes to avoid eating any kind of animal-derived foods. <P>And yes, you don't want to use magnum loads for skeet shooting; skeet calls for milder loads with smaller shot.  In a shotgun, magnum loads have more shot by weight and they shoot it with the same or slower speed than a standard load.   Magnum loads are more appropriate for things like goose or duck hunting. <P>Merry Christmas to all! <P>||||reg||1
